window.TL_PACKS = [{"id":"2026-11-18","week":1,"title":"Family first","level":"Understands some, speaks little","made":"2026-11-18, written by Claude for Braeden's first week","focus":"Polite Tagalog for his wife's family, talking about Inara (turning one on 22 Nov), and the core sentence patterns he half-recognises from listening.","lessons":[{"day":1,"title":"Po, opo and greeting elders","intro":"You already hear 'po' everywhere. It shows respect to anyone older: parents-in-law, Lola, aunties. Put it after the first word or phrase of what you say, and use 'kayo' (you, respectful) instead of 'ikaw'.","words":[{"tl":"po","en":"polite particle for elders (like sir / ma'am)"},{"tl":"opo","en":"yes (polite)"},{"tl":"hindi po","en":"no (polite)"},{"tl":"Magandang umaga po","en":"Good morning"},{"tl":"Magandang hapon po","en":"Good afternoon"},{"tl":"Magandang gabi po","en":"Good evening"},{"tl":"Kumusta po kayo?","en":"How are you? (respectful)"},{"tl":"Mabuti naman po","en":"I'm fine / doing well"},{"tl":"Mano po","en":"said while taking an elder's hand to your forehead (asking for a blessing)"},{"tl":"Salamat po","en":"Thank you"},{"tl":"Ingat po kayo","en":"Take care"}],"dialogue":[{"who":"You","tl":"Magandang hapon po, Lola. Mano po.","en":"Good afternoon, Grandma. (taking her hand for a blessing)"},{"who":"Lola","tl":"Kaawaan ka ng Diyos. Kumusta ka na?","en":"God bless you. How are you now?"},{"who":"You","tl":"Mabuti naman po. Kayo po?","en":"I'm fine. And you?"},{"who":"Lola","tl":"Mabuti rin. Nasaan si Inara?","en":"Fine too. Where's Inara?"},{"who":"You","tl":"Natutulog po siya.","en":"She's sleeping."}],"grammar":{"title":"Where 'po' goes","explain":"'Po' usually comes right after the first word or chunk: 'Salamat po', 'Kumusta po kayo?', 'Mabuti naman po'. 'Opo' is a polite 'oo' (yes). Use 'kayo' instead of 'ikaw' and 'nila' or 'ninyo' instead of 'mo' when you speak to elders. 'Ho' is a slightly more casual version you may hear.","examples":[{"tl":"Salamat po sa pagkain.","en":"Thank you for the food."},{"tl":"Opo, gising na po siya.","en":"Yes, she's awake now."}]},"culture":"Mano: when you arrive, take an elder's right hand and touch the back of it to your forehead while saying 'Mano po'. Families love seeing this, especially from a son-in-law.","exercises":[{"type":"mc","prompt":"What does 'opo' mean?","options":["yes (polite)","no","hello","thank you"],"answer":"yes (polite)"},{"type":"listen","say":"Magandang gabi po","options":["Good evening","Good morning","Good afternoon","Take care"],"answer":"Good evening"},{"type":"mc","prompt":"Lola asks 'Kumusta ka na?'. A polite answer:","options":["Mabuti naman po.","Mabuti naman.","Ingat ka.","Salamat."],"answer":"Mabuti naman po."},{"type":"build","prompt":"How are you? (to your mother-in-law)","words":["kayo?","Kumusta","po"],"answer":"Kumusta po kayo?"},{"type":"type","prompt":"Say 'Thank you' to an elder.","answer":"Salamat po","accept":["salamat po","salamat ho","maraming salamat po"]}]},{"day":2,"title":"Talking about the baby","intro":"Everyone will ask about Inara, especially with her first birthday on 22 November. These phrases cover most of it.","words":[{"tl":"anak","en":"child (son or daughter)"},{"tl":"sanggol","en":"baby (people often just say 'baby')"},{"tl":"natutulog","en":"is sleeping"},{"tl":"gising","en":"awake"},{"tl":"umiiyak","en":"is crying"},{"tl":"gutom","en":"hungry"},{"tl":"Ang cute!","en":"How cute!"},{"tl":"kamukha","en":"looks like (someone)"},{"tl":"buwan","en":"month"},{"tl":"kaarawan","en":"birthday (also 'birthday' in Taglish)"}],"dialogue":[{"who":"Tita","tl":"Ang cute ng anak mo! Ilang buwan na siya?","en":"Your child is so cute! How many months is she?"},{"who":"You","tl":"Mag-iisang taon na po siya.","en":"She's about to turn one."},{"who":"Tita","tl":"Kamukha mo siya!","en":"She looks like you!"},{"who":"You","tl":"Sabi nila, kamukha raw siya ng nanay niya.","en":"They say she looks like her mom."},{"who":"Tita","tl":"Gutom na ba siya?","en":"Is she hungry already?"},{"who":"You","tl":"Opo, kakain na po kami.","en":"Yes, we're about to eat."}],"grammar":{"title":"'Ang ...!' and 'na'","explain":"'Ang' + a describing word makes an exclamation: 'Ang cute!', 'Ang init!' (So hot!), 'Ang bigat niya!' (She's so heavy!). 'Na' after a word means 'already / now': 'Gising na siya' (She's awake now), 'Gutom na ba siya?' (Is she hungry yet?).","examples":[{"tl":"Ang laki na niya!","en":"She's so big now!"},{"tl":"Tulog na si Inara.","en":"Inara is asleep now."}]},"culture":"A baby's first birthday is a big celebration in Filipino families, often with lots of food and relatives. Expect the questions above many times.","exercises":[{"type":"mc","prompt":"'Umiiyak si Inara' means:","options":["Inara is crying","Inara is sleeping","Inara is eating","Inara is awake"],"answer":"Inara is crying"},{"type":"listen","say":"Ang cute ng anak mo!","options":["Your child is so cute!","Your child is hungry!","Your child is big!","Your child is sleeping!"],"answer":"Your child is so cute!"},{"type":"build","prompt":"She's awake now.","words":["siya.","na","Gising"],"answer":"Gising na siya."},{"type":"type","prompt":"How cute!","answer":"Ang cute!","accept":["ang cute","ang cute!"]},{"type":"mc","prompt":"Someone says 'Kamukha mo siya!'. They mean:","options":["She looks like you","She is like a month old","She is hungry","She is heavy"],"answer":"She looks like you"}]},{"day":3,"title":"Who does what: ang, ng, sa","intro":"Three little words carry a lot of meaning. Once you notice them, sentences you half understand will click into place.","words":[{"tl":"si / ang","en":"marks the main person or thing in the sentence (si for names)"},{"tl":"ni / ng","en":"of, or marks the thing being done-to (ni for names)"},{"tl":"kay / sa","en":"to, at, for (kay for names)"},{"tl":"saging","en":"banana"},{"tl":"gatas","en":"milk"},{"tl":"laruan","en":"toy"},{"tl":"bahay","en":"house"},{"tl":"pupunta","en":"will go"}],"dialogue":[{"who":"Wife","tl":"Kumakain si Inara ng saging.","en":"Inara is eating a banana."},{"who":"You","tl":"Nasaan ang laruan niya?","en":"Where's her toy?"},{"who":"Wife","tl":"Nasa sofa. Ibigay mo kay Inara.","en":"On the sofa. Give it to Inara."},{"who":"You","tl":"Pupunta tayo sa bahay ni Lola bukas?","en":"Are we going to Lola's house tomorrow?"},{"who":"Wife","tl":"Oo, sa hapon.","en":"Yes, in the afternoon."}],"grammar":{"title":"The three markers","explain":"In 'Kumakain si Inara ng saging': 'si' marks Inara (the one the sentence is about), 'ng' marks the banana (what she eats). 'Sa' marks places and directions: 'sa bahay' (at/to the house). For names use si / ni / kay: 'bahay ni Lola' (Lola's house), 'kay Inara' (to Inara). 'Ng' is pronounced 'nang'.","examples":[{"tl":"Umiinom ng gatas ang baby.","en":"The baby is drinking milk."},{"tl":"Nasa kusina si Tatay.","en":"Dad is in the kitchen."}]},"culture":"Taglish (mixing English in) is completely normal in the Philippines. 'Nasa sofa', 'ibigay mo sa baby': mixing is fine while you learn.","exercises":[{"type":"mc","prompt":"Kumakain ___ Inara ng saging.","options":["si","ng","sa","kay"],"answer":"si"},{"type":"mc","prompt":"Pupunta kami ___ bahay ni Lola.","options":["sa","si","ng","ang"],"answer":"sa"},{"type":"mc","prompt":"'bahay ni Lola' means:","options":["Lola's house","to Lola's house","Lola is at home","a house for Lola"],"answer":"Lola's house"},{"type":"build","prompt":"The baby is drinking milk.","words":["ang","baby.","Umiinom","ng","gatas"],"answer":"Umiinom ng gatas ang baby."},{"type":"listen","say":"Ibigay mo kay Inara.","options":["Give it to Inara.","Inara gave it.","Give Inara a bath.","Where is Inara?"],"answer":"Give it to Inara."}]},{"day":4,"title":"Did, doing, will do","intro":"Tagalog verbs show whether something happened, is happening, or will happen, by changing the front of the word. The '-um-' verbs are the easiest place to start.","words":[{"tl":"kumain / kumakain / kakain","en":"ate / is eating / will eat"},{"tl":"uminom / umiinom / iinom","en":"drank / is drinking / will drink"},{"tl":"umalis / umaalis / aalis","en":"left / is leaving / will leave"},{"tl":"kumanta / kumakanta / kakanta","en":"sang / is singing / will sing"},{"tl":"bukas","en":"tomorrow"},{"tl":"kanina","en":"earlier today"},{"tl":"ngayon","en":"now, today"}],"dialogue":[{"who":"Wife","tl":"Kumain ka na ba?","en":"Have you eaten yet?"},{"who":"You","tl":"Hindi pa. Kakain ako mamaya.","en":"Not yet. I'll eat later."},{"who":"Wife","tl":"Uminom na ng gatas si Inara kanina.","en":"Inara already drank milk earlier."},{"who":"You","tl":"Aalis tayo bukas ng umaga?","en":"Are we leaving tomorrow morning?"},{"who":"Wife","tl":"Oo. Kumakanta pa si Nanay, hintayin natin.","en":"Yes. Mom is still singing, let's wait for her."}],"grammar":{"title":"The -um- pattern","explain":"Take the root (kain, inom, alis). Done: put -um- after the first consonant: k-um-ain = kumain; a root starting with a vowel takes um- in front: uminom. Ongoing: the done form, but repeat the first syllable of the root: kumakain, umiinom. Future: just repeat the first syllable: kakain, iinom, aalis.","examples":[{"tl":"Kumakain ako.","en":"I'm eating."},{"tl":"Iinom siya ng tubig.","en":"He/she will drink water."}]},"culture":"'Kumain ka na?' (Have you eaten?) is also an everyday greeting and a way of showing care, a bit like 'How are you?'.","exercises":[{"type":"mc","prompt":"'kakain' means:","options":["will eat","ate","is eating","food"],"answer":"will eat"},{"type":"mc","prompt":"Inara is drinking milk: ___ ng gatas si Inara.","options":["Umiinom","Uminom","Iinom","Inom"],"answer":"Umiinom"},{"type":"type","prompt":"Make 'kanta' (sing) future: will sing","answer":"kakanta","accept":["kakanta"]},{"type":"listen","say":"Kumain ka na ba?","options":["Have you eaten yet?","Are you eating now?","Will you eat?","Do you want food?"],"answer":"Have you eaten yet?"},{"type":"build","prompt":"Not yet. I'll eat later.","words":["mamaya.","pa.","Kakain","Hindi","ako"],"answer":"Hindi pa. Kakain ako mamaya."}]},{"day":5,"title":"At the family table","intro":"Filipino gatherings centre on food, and the polite way to accept, praise and refuse it matters.","words":[{"tl":"Kain tayo!","en":"Let's eat! (an invitation)"},{"tl":"Masarap!","en":"Delicious!"},{"tl":"Busog na ako","en":"I'm full"},{"tl":"Gusto ko pa","en":"I'd like more"},{"tl":"Tama na po","en":"That's enough, thank you"},{"tl":"Pakiabot po ng kanin","en":"Please pass the rice"},{"tl":"kanin","en":"cooked rice"},{"tl":"ulam","en":"the dish eaten with rice"},{"tl":"Sino ang nagluto?","en":"Who cooked?"}],"dialogue":[{"who":"Tita","tl":"Kain tayo! Kumuha ka pa.","en":"Let's eat! Take some more."},{"who":"You","tl":"Salamat po. Ang sarap ng adobo! Sino po ang nagluto?","en":"Thank you. The adobo is so good! Who cooked?"},{"who":"Tita","tl":"Ako! Gusto mo pa?","en":"Me! Do you want more?"},{"who":"You","tl":"Busog na po ako, pero ang sarap talaga.","en":"I'm full, but it's really delicious."}],"grammar":{"title":"pa and na","explain":"'Pa' means 'still / more / yet': 'Gusto ko pa' (I want more), 'Hindi pa' (not yet). 'Na' means 'already / now / anymore': 'Busog na ako' (I'm full now), 'Ayoko na' (I don't want any more). 'Paki-' at the start of a verb means 'please': 'Pakiabot' (please pass), 'Pakihawak' (please hold).","examples":[{"tl":"Pakihawak po si Inara.","en":"Please hold Inara."},{"tl":"Kain pa!","en":"Eat more!"}]},"culture":"Hosts will insist you eat more. Praising the food and the cook ('Ang sarap po!') is the best compliment; 'Busog na po ako' with a smile is a polite way to stop.","exercises":[{"type":"mc","prompt":"Tita offers more food and you're full. Best answer:","options":["Busog na po ako, salamat.","Ayoko.","Hindi masarap.","Gusto ko pa."],"answer":"Busog na po ako, salamat."},{"type":"listen","say":"Pakiabot po ng kanin.","options":["Please pass the rice.","The rice is delicious.","Who cooked the rice?","I want more rice."],"answer":"Please pass the rice."},{"type":"type","prompt":"Delicious!","answer":"Masarap!","accept":["masarap","ang sarap","masarap!"]},{"type":"mc","prompt":"'Hindi pa' means:","options":["not yet","not anymore","no thanks","not here"],"answer":"not yet"},{"type":"build","prompt":"Please hold Inara.","words":["si","po","Inara.","Pakihawak"],"answer":"Pakihawak po si Inara."}]},{"day":6,"title":"Asking questions","intro":"With a handful of question words you can keep a conversation going, and show interest in the family.","words":[{"tl":"Ano?","en":"What?"},{"tl":"Sino?","en":"Who?"},{"tl":"Nasaan?","en":"Where is ...?"},{"tl":"Saan?","en":"Where (to / at, with an action)?"},{"tl":"Kailan?","en":"When?"},{"tl":"Bakit?","en":"Why?"},{"tl":"Paano?","en":"How?"},{"tl":"Ilan?","en":"How many?"},{"tl":"Magkano?","en":"How much (price)?"}],"dialogue":[{"who":"You","tl":"Nasaan po si Lolo?","en":"Where's Grandpa?"},{"who":"Tita","tl":"Nasa labas, nagdidilig.","en":"Outside, watering the plants."},{"who":"You","tl":"Kailan po kayo uuwi sa Pilipinas?","en":"When are you going home to the Philippines?"},{"who":"Tita","tl":"Sa Disyembre. Bakit, gusto mong sumama?","en":"In December. Why, do you want to come along?"},{"who":"You","tl":"Gusto ko po! Paano po ba?","en":"I'd love to! How does it work?"}],"grammar":{"title":"Nasaan vs saan","explain":"'Nasaan' asks where something or someone IS: 'Nasaan ang bote?' (Where's the bottle?). 'Saan' goes with an action: 'Saan tayo kakain?' (Where will we eat?). Answer 'nasaan' with 'nasa': 'Nasa kusina' (In the kitchen).","examples":[{"tl":"Bakit siya umiiyak?","en":"Why is she crying?"},{"tl":"Ilan ang anak ninyo?","en":"How many children do you have?"}]},"culture":"Asking elders about their hometown in the Philippines ('Taga-saan po kayo?', Where are you from?) is a warm way to start a long conversation.","exercises":[{"type":"mc","prompt":"Where's the bottle?","options":["Nasaan ang bote?","Saan ang bote?","Ano ang bote?","Kailan ang bote?"],"answer":"Nasaan ang bote?"},{"type":"listen","say":"Bakit siya umiiyak?","options":["Why is she crying?","Who is crying?","When did she cry?","Is she crying?"],"answer":"Why is she crying?"},{"type":"mc","prompt":"'Taga-saan po kayo?' asks:","options":["Where are you from?","Where are you going?","When did you arrive?","Who are you?"],"answer":"Where are you from?"},{"type":"type","prompt":"When?","answer":"Kailan?","accept":["kailan"]},{"type":"build","prompt":"Where will we eat?","words":["kakain?","tayo","Saan"],"answer":"Saan tayo kakain?"}]},{"day":7,"title":"Linking words, and the week together","intro":"One last pattern explains a sound you hear constantly: the -ng or 'na' that joins describing words to things. Then a review of the week.","words":[{"tl":"magandang umaga","en":"good morning (maganda + ng)"},{"tl":"mabait na bata","en":"a well-behaved / kind child"},{"tl":"masayang pamilya","en":"a happy family"},{"tl":"malaking bahay","en":"a big house"},{"tl":"maliit na kamay","en":"a small hand"},{"tl":"mabait","en":"kind, good-natured"}],"dialogue":[{"who":"Lola","tl":"Ang bait ng baby mo. Hindi umiiyak!","en":"Your baby is so good. She doesn't cry!"},{"who":"You","tl":"Mabait na bata po si Inara. Pero umiiyak din po siya sa gabi!","en":"Inara is a good kid. But she cries at night too!"},{"who":"Lola","tl":"Ganyan talaga. Masayang pamilya kayo.","en":"That's how it is. You're a happy family."},{"who":"You","tl":"Salamat po, Lola.","en":"Thank you, Lola."}],"grammar":{"title":"-ng and na","explain":"When a describing word sits next to a noun, a linker joins them. If the first word ends in a vowel, add -ng: maganda + umaga = magandang umaga, masaya + pamilya = masayang pamilya. If it ends in n, the n becomes -ng. Otherwise put 'na' between the two words: mabait na bata, maliit na kamay.","examples":[{"tl":"Masarap na pagkain","en":"delicious food"},{"tl":"Malaking pasasalamat po","en":"Big thanks"}]},"culture":"Over this week you've practised the words that matter most at a family gathering: respect (po, mano), the baby, and food. Try one phrase at the next visit; your effort will be noticed and loved.","exercises":[{"type":"mc","prompt":"a kind child:","options":["mabait na bata","mabaitng bata","bata mabait","mabait ang bata ng"],"answer":"mabait na bata"},{"type":"type","prompt":"Good morning (to an elder)","answer":"Magandang umaga po","accept":["magandang umaga po","magandang umaga ho"]},{"type":"listen","say":"Masayang pamilya kayo.","options":["You're a happy family.","Your family is big.","Where is your family?","Your family is eating."],"answer":"You're a happy family."},{"type":"mc","prompt":"She will eat later:","options":["Kakain siya mamaya.","Kumain siya mamaya.","Kumakain siya kanina.","Kain siya mamaya."],"answer":"Kakain siya mamaya."},{"type":"build","prompt":"Inara is a good kid.","words":["si","bata","Mabait","na","Inara."],"answer":"Mabait na bata si Inara."}]}]}];
